<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/intermediate/elasticity">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    When price rises, demand falls — but <strong>by how much?</strong> Elasticity is the answer:
    the percentage change in quantity per percentage change in price. <strong>Elastic</strong>
    goods (holidays, restaurant meals) shed buyers fast; <strong>inelastic</strong> goods
    (insulin, salt, fuel) barely flinch. The slope of the curve — literally — decides who bears
    a tax and whether a price rise earns money.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two demands, two personalities — raise price and watch which market walks away" widget="plotter" config={elascfg} />

  <p>
    The steep (inelastic) curve barely moves when price rises: sellers can pass taxes to buyers
    almost fully — which is why fuel taxes work and why insulin pricing scandalises economists.
    The flat (elastic) curve punishes any increase: luxury sellers eat their own taxes.
  </p>

  <h2>The elasticities worth knowing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Price elasticity of demand:</strong> measured by availability of substitutes, necessity, and share of income. Necessities with no substitutes: inelastic. Everything else: somewhere in between.</li>
    <li><strong>Income elasticity:</strong> normal goods rise with income; inferior goods (instant noodles) fall — why recessions oddly help some brands.</li>
    <li><strong>Cross elasticity:</strong> how demand for one good responds to another's price — positive for substitutes (Coke vs Pepsi), negative for complements (cars and fuel).</li>
    <li><strong>Time matters:</strong> elasticity rises with time horizons — fuel demand is inelastic this month, more elastic over years as people buy efficient cars. Policy that works short-term fails long-term.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Marshall, Principles of Economics (1890); OpenStax, Principles of Economics (Elasticity).</p>
</TopicPage>
